This keeps the linked list setup small. `head` points to the next item to leave, while `tail` marks where a new item should be attached. After removing the last item, both pointers must be reset, which is the part that is easiest to miss.

For example, after `push(4)`, `push(7)`, and `pop()`, the list changes from `4 -> 7` to just `7`, and the returned value is `4`.

```python
class LinkedListQueue:
    class Node:
        def __init__(self, value):
            self.value = value
            self.next = None

    def __init__(self):
        self.head = None
        self.tail = None

    def push(self, x):
        new_node = self.Node(x)

        if self.tail is None:
            self.head = new_node
            self.tail = new_node
        else:
            self.tail.next = new_node
            self.tail = new_node

    def pop(self):
        if self.head is None:
            return -1

        value = self.head.value
        self.head = self.head.next

        if self.head is None:
            self.tail = None

        return value

    def peek(self):
        if self.head is None:
            return -1
        return self.head.value

    def isEmpty(self):
        return self.head is None
```

The empty queue is handled by returning `-1` from `pop()` and `peek()`. Every operation is `O(1)`, and storing `n` nodes takes `O(n)` space. The input is not modified.

A queue needs to add at the back and remove from the front. I keep two pointers, `front` and `rear`, so both operations can be done without walking through the list. The list owns each node, and `pop()` returns `-1` when there is nothing to remove.

```cpp
class LinkedListQueue {
private:
    struct Node {
        int value;
        Node* next;

        Node(int value) : value(value), next(nullptr) {
        }
    };

    Node* frontNode;
    Node* rearNode;

public:
    LinkedListQueue() : frontNode(nullptr), rearNode(nullptr) {
    }

    void push(int x) {
        Node* newNode = new Node(x);

        if (rearNode == nullptr) {
            frontNode = newNode;
            rearNode = newNode;
            return;
        }

        rearNode->next = newNode;
        rearNode = newNode;
    }

    int pop() {
        if (frontNode == nullptr) {
            return -1;
        }

        Node* oldFront = frontNode;
        int value = oldFront->value;
        frontNode = frontNode->next;

        if (frontNode == nullptr) {
            rearNode = nullptr;
        }

        delete oldFront;
        return value;
    }

    int peek() {
        if (frontNode == nullptr) {
            return -1;
        }

        return frontNode->value;
    }

    bool isEmpty() {
        return frontNode == nullptr;
    }
};
```

Each `push`, `pop`, and `peek` takes `O(1)` time. The queue uses `O(n)` space for its nodes. The input is not changed.

This is the more direct singly linked version. It stores only the first node and walks to the end whenever a new value is added. That makes the code easy to follow, but `push()` is slower than the versions that keep a tail pointer.

The empty case is checked before reading a node. In this implementation, `-1` represents an unavailable value for `pop()` or `peek()`.

```java
class LinkedListQueue {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        Node next;

        Node(int value) {
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    private Node head;

    public LinkedListQueue() {
        head = null;
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        Node newNode = new Node(x);

        if (head == null) {
            head = newNode;
            return;
        }

        Node current = head;
        while (current.next != null) {
            current = current.next;
        }

        current.next = newNode;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (head == null) {
            return -1;
        }

        int value = head.value;
        head = head.next;
        return value;
    }

    public int peek() {
        if (head == null) {
            return -1;
        }

        return head.value;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return head == null;
    }
}
```

With values `5`, `6`, and `7`, the list is `5 -> 6 -> 7`, so `pop()` returns `5` first. If the queue has one value, removing it sets `head` back to `null`.

`push()` takes `O(n)` because it may scan the list. `pop()`, `peek()`, and `isEmpty()` take `O(1)`. The linked list uses `O(n)` space. The input is not changed.

A dummy first node makes the empty and non-empty cases a little less mixed together. The real first element is always after `dummy`, and `tail` points to the last real node. When the queue becomes empty, `tail` is moved back to the dummy node.

The dummy node is never returned. It only gives the queue a fixed starting point.

```java
class LinkedListQueue {
    private static class Node {
        int value;
        Node next;

        Node(int value) {
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    private final Node dummy;
    private Node tail;

    public LinkedListQueue() {
        dummy = new Node(0);
        tail = dummy;
    }

    public void push(int x) {
        Node newNode = new Node(x);
        tail.next = newNode;
        tail = newNode;
    }

    public int pop() {
        if (dummy.next == null) {
            return -1;
        }

        Node first = dummy.next;
        dummy.next = first.next;

        if (dummy.next == null) {
            tail = dummy;
        }

        return first.value;
    }

    public int peek() {
        if (dummy.next == null) {
            return -1;
        }

        return dummy.next.value;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return dummy.next == null;
    }
}
```

A quick check is `push(10)`, `push(20)`, `pop()`, `peek()`: the results are `10` and then `20`. An empty queue returns `-1` for `pop()` and `peek()`.

All queue operations run in `O(1)` time. The linked nodes use `O(n)` space, plus one constant-size dummy node. The input is not changed.

Here the last node points back to the first node. I only store the rear pointer. The front is always `rear->next`, so there is no separate front pointer to update. For the first insertion, the node points to itself.

The important edge case is removing the only node. After that removal, `rear` must become `nullptr`; otherwise the queue would still look non-empty.

```cpp
class LinkedListQueue {
private:
    struct Node {
        int value;
        Node* next;

        Node(int value) : value(value), next(nullptr) {
        }
    };

    Node* rearNode;

public:
    LinkedListQueue() : rearNode(nullptr) {
    }

    void push(int x) {
        Node* newNode = new Node(x);

        if (rearNode == nullptr) {
            newNode->next = newNode;
            rearNode = newNode;
            return;
        }

        newNode->next = rearNode->next;
        rearNode->next = newNode;
        rearNode = newNode;
    }

    int pop() {
        if (rearNode == nullptr) {
            return -1;
        }

        Node* frontNode = rearNode->next;
        int value = frontNode->value;

        if (frontNode == rearNode) {
            rearNode = nullptr;
        } else {
            rearNode->next = frontNode->next;
        }

        delete frontNode;
        return value;
    }

    int peek() {
        if (rearNode == nullptr) {
            return -1;
        }

        return rearNode->next->value;
    }

    bool isEmpty() {
        return rearNode == nullptr;
    }
};
```

For `push(1)`, `push(2)`, and `push(3)`, the circle is `1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 1`. The rear is `3`, so `rear->next` gives `1`, the correct front.

Every operation is `O(1)`. The nodes take `O(n)` space. This code changes only its own linked nodes and does not change any input array.
